# PROJECT CONTEXT

This repository contains my personal portfolio website hosted on GitHub Pages.

The main goal of the portfolio is to showcase my software development skills, professional experience, projects, and personality through a modern, original, and highly polished experience.

The portfolio also contains a 2D Interactive Museum.

The 2D Interactive Museum is a playable representation of the portfolio. Visitors should be able to explore a top-down 2D environment and discover projects through rooms, exhibits, objects, and interactive elements.

The museum should feel like a small polished video game that happens to function as a professional portfolio.

It must NOT feel like:

- A technical Phaser demo.
- A generic RPG template.
- A website with a character walking over it.
- A collection of unrelated pixel-art assets.
- A rushed prototype.

The visual direction may be inspired by the cozy feeling of classic top-down pixel-art games and Stardew Valley interiors. However, the museum must develop its own visual identity and must not directly copy copyrighted art, characters, maps, interfaces, or assets.


# REPOSITORY STRUCTURE

This repository functions as a hub.

The following files and folders belong to the main portfolio:

- index.html
- laboratory.html
- css/
- js/
- img/

The following folders are independent projects, laboratories, tools, or documents:

- 4yax/
- ask/
- Economy_Exam/
- docs/
- yaml_cv/

STRICT RULE:

Do not read, analyze, search, modify, refactor, or otherwise interact with the contents of independent sub-project folders unless the user explicitly asks to work on one of them.

By default, assume that work is limited to the Main Portfolio and files directly related to the 2D Interactive Museum.


# TECHNOLOGY

The portfolio uses:

- HTML5.
- Vanilla JavaScript ES6+.
- Modern CSS.

Use CSS Grid, Flexbox, and CSS variables when appropriate.

Do not use inline styles.

Do not introduce a new framework unless explicitly approved.

Phaser 3 may be used for the 2D Interactive Museum.


# USER INTERFACE LANGUAGE

All text rendered to the user must be in English.

Internal documentation and comments may be written in English or Spanish when useful.


# UI AND UX

The final experience should feel:

- Premium.
- Intentional.
- Coherent.
- Responsive.
- Polished.
- Visually memorable.

Do not add animations, effects, sounds, or interactions merely because they are technically possible.

Every effect should have a clear purpose.


# IMPORTANT DECISION RULE

Never silently invent important project decisions.

If an important decision has not been defined, stop and ask the user.

Examples include:

- Tile size.
- Pixel-art resolution.
- Character dimensions.
- Character proportions.
- Camera zoom.
- Perspective.
- World scale.
- Color palette.
- Lighting.
- Shadows.
- Outlines.
- Tileset.
- Spritesheet.
- Asset strategy.
- Map architecture.
- Room layout.
- Navigation flow.
- Interaction model.
- UI style.
- Modal design.
- Audio direction.
- External asset licensing.

When an important decision is undefined:

1. Identify the decision.
2. Explain briefly why it matters.
3. Present reasonable alternatives.
4. Give a recommendation when appropriate.
5. Wait for explicit user approval.

The absence of a decision does not constitute permission to choose automatically.


# PROJECT DOCUMENTATION

The project contains three documentation files:

- PLAN.md
- DECISIONS.md
- ARCHITECTURE.md

Each file has a different purpose.

PLAN.md defines what needs to be done and the current development phase.

DECISIONS.md defines which important project decisions have been approved and why.

ARCHITECTURE.md defines how the technical system is structured.

These files are the project memory.

Chat history is not considered a reliable source of truth.

If chat history conflicts with these documents, treat the documents as authoritative unless the user explicitly states otherwise.


# SESSION RECOVERY RULE (CONTEXT INITIALIZATION)

Whenever a new chat session starts, the AI MUST immediately read `PLAN.md`, `DECISIONS.md`, and `ARCHITECTURE.md` before taking any action or proposing any solution. 
This guarantees that the AI recovers the project state and does not rely on its temporal memory or hallucinate decisions.


# DECISION LOG RULE

Every important user-approved decision must be recorded in DECISIONS.md before implementation.

This applies to decisions involving:

- Architecture.
- Gameplay.
- Visual direction.
- Assets.
- UI.
- Map design.
- Technical implementation.
- Performance.
- Scope.
- Project structure.

Do not rely on memory of previous conversations.

Only explicitly approved decisions should be recorded as APPROVED decisions.


# ARCHITECTURE DOCUMENTATION RULE

When an approved decision changes the technical architecture, update ARCHITECTURE.md.

Examples include:

- Phaser scene structure.
- Tilemap system.
- Asset loading system.
- Spritesheet system.
- Texture atlas system.
- Collision system.
- Camera system.
- Interaction system.
- Data structures.
- External dependencies.


# DEVELOPMENT WORKFLOW

The project is developed according to PLAN.md.

Development is divided into sequential phases and GATEs.

The AI may:

- Audit.
- Research.
- Analyze.
- Compare alternatives.
- Propose solutions.
- Prepare documentation.

The AI must not implement the next phase without explicit user approval.

If the current phase has not been approved, stop.

If a new important decision appears during implementation, stop and ask the user.


# VISUAL APPROVAL RULE

Any important visual decision requires explicit user approval before definitive integration.

This includes:

- Pixel-art style.
- Tilesets.
- Sprites.
- Spritesheets.
- Characters.
- Tiles.
- Palette.
- Perspective.
- Architecture.
- Furniture.
- Decoration.
- Lighting.
- Shadows.
- UI.
- Animations.

Temporary prototypes may be created when necessary for comparison.

Temporary prototypes must remain clearly identified as temporary.

A temporary prototype must not become a definitive asset without approval.


# ASSET RULES

Phaser 3 is a game framework and does not automatically provide a complete library of game art.

Before replacing or creating graphics, determine where the current graphics come from.

Possible sources include:

- PNG files.
- JPG files.
- WebP files.
- SVG files.
- Spritesheets.
- Texture atlases.
- Tilemaps.
- Procedurally generated graphics.
- Phaser Graphics objects.
- External assets.
- AI-generated assets.

Procedural graphics may be used for temporary prototyping.

Procedural graphics must not automatically become the final visual solution.


# ASSET CONSISTENCY

Never select individual assets without checking whether they belong to the same visual system.

Check:

- Resolution.
- Tile size.
- Scale.
- Perspective.
- Character proportions.
- Palette.
- Lighting.
- Shadows.
- Outlines.
- Detail level.
- Overall visual language.

The character must look like it belongs in the environment.

Furniture must look like it belongs in the building.

Objects must look like they belong in the same world.

The UI must belong to the same overall experience.


# LICENSING

For every external asset, determine its license before integrating it.

Check whether it:

- Is free to use.
- Requires attribution.
- Allows commercial use.
- Allows modification.
- Has restrictions.
- Has an unknown license.

Never assume that an image found online is free to use.


# CLEAN CODE

When replacing a temporary system with a definitive solution:

- Remove dead code.
- Remove mocks.
- Remove temporary generators.
- Remove unused assets.
- Remove unused imports.
- Remove abandoned systems.
- Remove obsolete files.

Do not preserve obsolete code just in case.

Before deleting a file, check its references and dependencies.


# CODE MARKERS

Only use these markers:

TODO:
FIXME:
DELETEME:
WAITING:
README:

TODO means pending implementation or future work.

FIXME means a known bug or problematic temporary implementation.

DELETEME means temporary or test code that must be removed before production.

WAITING means something depends on external configuration or user approval.

README means an important explanation of complex functionality.

Markers must always use the correct comment syntax for the file type.

Do not invent other marker types.


# BEFORE MODIFYING CODE

Before writing or refactoring code:

1. Explain the implementation approach briefly.
2. Identify the files that will be modified.
3. Identify the approved decision being implemented.
4. Confirm that the change belongs to the current PLAN.md phase.
5. Implement only the approved scope.

Do not perform unrelated refactors.


# WHEN A NEW DECISION APPEARS

If implementation reveals an important decision that was not previously defined:

STOP.

Do not silently choose.

Instead:

1. Explain the decision.
2. Explain its impact.
3. Present reasonable alternatives.
4. Ask for user approval.
5. Record the approved decision in DECISIONS.md.
6. Update ARCHITECTURE.md if the decision affects technical architecture.
7. Only then implement it.


# GIT SAFETY

The repository uses Git.

Before large or potentially destructive changes:

- Check Git status.
- Avoid destructive operations without explicit approval.
- Do not reset or rewrite Git history unless explicitly requested.
- Do not delete large portions of the project blindly.

Prefer small, logically grouped commits during major milestones.


# QUALITY PRIORITIES

When making design or implementation decisions, prioritize:

1. Coherence.
2. User experience.
3. Visual quality.
4. Maintainability.
5. Performance.
6. Development speed.

Do not sacrifice visual coherence simply to implement something faster.


# FINAL PRODUCT PRINCIPLE

The 2D Interactive Museum should feel like a small polished exploration game that happens to be a professional portfolio.

The visitor should remember the experience, not the technology used to build it.